menu "USB-PD trigger (HUSB238A)"

    config HUSB238A
        bool "HUSB238A USB-PD trigger on the DAC's I2C bus"
        depends on DAC_TAS58XX
        default n
        help
            Look for a Hynetek HUSB238A, strapped for I2C at 0x42, on the
            DAC's bus, and ask the USB-C charger for a higher voltage than
            the 5 V every port gives. The amplifier runs from that supply,
            so a higher one lets it play louder before it clips, and full
            volume is raised to use it.

            Strapped for I2C, the chip asks for nothing until it is told to,
            and forgets on every power loss, so it is asked again on every
            boot, before the amplifier comes up. A board without the chip
            does not answer at its address and is left alone, so one build
            can serve boards with and without it, such as the 42 mm and the
            55 mm Louder-ESP32-Mini.

    config HUSB238A_DEFAULT_VOLTS
        int "Voltage to ask for until one is chosen on the settings page"
        depends on HUSB238A
        range 5 20
        default 5
        help
            One of the fixed USB-PD offers: 5, 9, 12, 15 or 20 V. Anything
            else falls to the highest of those below it, and so does a
            voltage the charger does not offer.

    config HUSB238A_MAX_VOLTS
        int "Highest voltage the board's supply rail is rated for"
        depends on HUSB238A
        range 5 20
        default 20
        help
            Never asked for, whatever is chosen. The chip can also negotiate
            the 28-48 V EPR offers; those are never asked for at all.

endmenu
